\section{Data}{

Every estimation function accepts the data in three forms:
\itemize{
\item one trajectory: \code{f(x, t)}, with \code{x} the degradation levels and \code{t} the
inspection times;
\item several trajectories with the same inspection times: \code{f(x)}, with \code{x} the
matrix returned by the \code{levysim} simulation functions (columns \code{t}, \code{X1},
\code{X2}, ...);
\item several trajectories, each with its own inspection times: \code{f(liste)},
with \code{liste} a list of data frames, one per trajectory, whose first column
contains the inspection times and second column the degradation levels.
}

The trajectories are assumed independent and with the same law. For the
Lévy processes, the increments are computed within each trajectory and then
pooled; for the inverse gamma process, which is not a Lévy process, the
log-likelihoods of the trajectories are added.
}

\section{Computation of the estimators}{

With \eqn{(\Delta t_j, \Delta x_j)}, \eqn{j = 1, \dots, m}, the durations and
increments between two consecutive inspections, pooled over the
trajectories, the log-likelihood of a Lévy process is
\eqn{\ell(\psi) = \sum_j \ln f_{\Delta t_j}(\Delta x_j; \psi)}.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Closed form}: Wiener and inverse Gaussian. The MLE is explicit, with
\eqn{\hat\mu = S / T}, \eqn{S} the total degradation and \eqn{T} the total
observed time.
\item \strong{Root of one equation}: gamma. \eqn{\hat b = \hat a T / S} is explicit
and \eqn{\hat a} is the unique root of a decreasing function, found by
\code{\link[stats:uniroot]{stats::uniroot()}} from the method of moments estimator; the result does
not depend on this starting point.
\item \strong{Numerical maximization}: variance-gamma and Meixner.
\itemize{
\item Variance-gamma: EM algorithm, the gamma time change being the hidden
variable (each step is explicit or a unique root), started from the
Wiener MLE, then L-BFGS-B (\code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optim()}}) with a numerical gradient.
\item Meixner: L-BFGS-B on the likelihood profiled in \eqn{b} (\eqn{\hat b} is
explicit for given \eqn{a}, \eqn{d}, \eqn{m}), with an explicit gradient
and several starting points (\code{n_start}) built from the empirical mean
and variance; the best interior maximum is kept.
}
}

When the supremum of the likelihood is reached at the boundary of the
parameter space (for instance the Brownian limit of the variance-gamma
process), the MLE does not exist: the function gives a warning and returns
\code{NA}. The help page of each function details its derivation.
}

\section{Confidence intervals}{

With \code{ci = TRUE}, the intervals are Wald intervals. The observed Fisher
information \eqn{J = -\nabla^2 \ell(\hat\psi)} is computed numerically by
\code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optimHess()}} for every model, and the standard error of
\eqn{\hat\psi_k} is \eqn{\widehat{se}_k = \sqrt{(J^{-1})_{kk}}}. With
\eqn{z} the quantile of order \eqn{(1 + level) / 2} of the standard normal
distribution:
\itemize{
\item real parameter (\code{mu} of the Wiener process, \code{theta} of the variance-gamma
process, \code{b} and \code{m} of the Meixner process):
\eqn{\hat\psi_k \pm z\, \widehat{se}_k};
\item positive parameter (all the others): the interval
\eqn{\ln \hat\psi_k \pm z\, \widehat{se}_k / \hat\psi_k} for
\eqn{\ln \psi_k} (delta method), transformed back:
\eqn{[\hat\psi_k e^{-z \widehat{se}_k / \hat\psi_k},\ \hat\psi_k e^{z \widehat{se}_k / \hat\psi_k}]},
which always stays positive.
}

For the Wiener, inverse Gaussian and gamma processes, the numerical
information agrees with its closed form; for instance, for the Wiener
process, \eqn{\widehat{se}(\hat\mu) = \hat\sigma / \sqrt{T}} and
\eqn{\widehat{se}(\hat\sigma^2) = \hat\sigma^2 \sqrt{2 / m}}.
}

\section{Parametrizations}{

\itemize{
\item Wiener: \eqn{X_t - X_s \sim N(\mu (t - s), \sigma^2 (t - s))}.
\item Gamma: \eqn{X_t - X_s \sim \Gamma(a (t - s), b)}, shape \eqn{a(t - s)} and
\strong{rate} \eqn{b}, so that \eqn{E[X_t] = a t / b}. The scale is \eqn{1 / b}.
\item Inverse Gaussian: \eqn{X_t - X_s \sim IG(\mu (t - s), \eta (t - s)^2)}.
\item Variance-gamma: \eqn{X_s = \theta \gamma_s + \sigma W_{\gamma_s}}, with
\eqn{\gamma_s} of mean \eqn{s} and variance \eqn{\nu s}.
\item Meixner: \eqn{X_s \sim Meixner(a, b, d s, m s)}.
\item Inverse gamma: \eqn{W(t) = \inf\{w : T(w) > t\}}, with \eqn{T} a gamma
process of shape \eqn{(w / \alpha)^\beta} and scale \eqn{\theta}.
}
}

